course: biology
lesson: 20
title: Leopards, jaguars, cheetahs and the house cat
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The cat on your sofa is a tiny cousin of the leopard: the same soft paws, sharp claws, whiskers and night-shining eyes, the same creeping, pouncing hunter inside.
explain: Each wild cat has its own speciality: the leopard is the strong climber that drags its meal up a tree, the jaguar has the crushing bite that cracks turtle shells, and the cheetah is the sprinter, the fastest runner on land.
explain: Spots tell them apart: leopard rosettes are small and empty in the middle, jaguar rosettes are bigger with spots inside, and the cheetah has plain solid spots and black tear lines down its face.
explain: A black panther is not a separate animal but a dark leopard or jaguar, and in bright light you can still see its spots under the black.
explain: House cats came from the African wildcat about 10,000 years ago, when farmers welcomed the mouse-catchers into their grain stores, and they still knead, purr, hunt and twist in the air to land on their feet.
fact: Leopards drag their kills, sometimes as heavy as themselves, up into trees where lions and hyenas cannot steal them.
fact: Leopard rosettes are small and empty in the middle; jaguar rosettes are bigger with spots inside; leopards live in Africa and Asia, jaguars only in the Americas.
fact: Jaguars have one of the strongest bites of any cat for their size, strong enough to crack turtle shells, and they catch caimans, small crocodiles, in rivers.
fact: A black panther is a leopard or jaguar with extra dark pigment, and its spots can still be seen in bright light.
fact: The cheetah is the fastest land animal, reaching about 100 kilometres per hour in a sprint with long legs, a light body, a bendy spine and a steering tail, but only for about 20 to 30 seconds before it overheats.
fact: Cheetahs cannot roar; they chirp, purr and hiss, and cubs answer their mother's chirp so she can find them in the grass.
fact: Cheetahs have dark tear lines from eyes to mouth that may cut the glare of the sun, and they hunt in the day, while most big cats hunt at night.
fact: Cats knead soft things with their paws, as kittens knead their mother for milk, and purr when happy, but sometimes also when hurt or scared.
fact: House cats come from the African wildcat; people began living with them about 10,000 years ago in the Middle East, and a cat was buried with a person on Cyprus about 9,500 years ago.
fact: Cat whiskers are very sensitive, about as wide as the cat's body, so they tell the cat whether it will fit through a gap, and help it sense prey in the dark.
fact: Cats twist in the air to land on their feet, the righting reflex, but it is a myth that they are always safe when they fall, so keep windows and balconies closed.
fact: Pet cats are still hunters even when well fed, because hunting is an instinct, something they are born with, from their wild ancestors, and they catch many birds and small animals, so a bell or keeping cats in at night helps wildlife.
fact: A house cat usually has 18 toes, 5 on each front paw and 4 on each back paw, and about 24 big whiskers on its face, about 12 on each side.
exercise: Why does a leopard drag its kill up a tree? | So lions and hyenas cannot steal it | To let the meat dry out in the hot sun | To hide it from vultures underground | To keep the meat cool in the shade of leaves
exercise: How can you tell a jaguar's spots from a leopard's? | Jaguar rosettes are bigger with spots inside | Jaguar spots join up into long stripes instead | Leopard rosettes have spots inside | They are exactly the same
exercise: Where do jaguars live? | Only in the Americas | In Africa and Asia only | Only in the forests of India | In the snowy Arctic and Siberia
exercise: What is special about a jaguar's bite? | One of the strongest of any cat for its size, cracking turtle shells | It is poisonous like a snake's | It has no teeth at all, so it can only give a soft and harmless squeeze | It is the weakest of all cats
exercise: What is a black panther? | A leopard or jaguar with very dark fur | A separate kind of black cat from Africa | A black tiger that was born with no stripes | A puma that has rolled about in dark mud
exercise: How fast can a cheetah run? | About 100 kilometres per hour in a sprint | About 10 kilometres per hour, like a jogger | About 300 kilometres per hour | As fast as a car on a motorway all day
exercise: How long can a cheetah keep its top speed? | Only about 20 to 30 seconds | For about an hour or more | All day long | Only about 2 seconds
exercise: What sounds does a cheetah make? | Chirps, purrs and hisses, but no roar | Deep, loud roars like a lion's, heard far across the plains | Barks like a dog | It makes no sound at all
exercise: What may a cheetah's black tear lines do? | Cut the glare of the sun so it sees prey in daylight | Show that it is sad | Help it smell its prey better in the hot midday sun | Scare off lions
exercise: When do cheetahs hunt? | In the day, unlike most big cats | Only at midnight, in total dark | Only in the rain | Only in winter
exercise: Why do cats knead a soft blanket? | Kittens knead their mother for milk and adults keep the habit | They are trying to dig a deep hole to sleep in, as wild cats do in sand | They are sharpening their teeth | They are counting the threads
exercise: Do cats purr only when they are happy? | No, sometimes also when hurt or scared | Yes, only ever when happy | No, only when hungry | Yes, and only when they are being stroked
exercise: Where do house cats come from? | The African wildcat, about 10,000 years ago | The tiger, only about 100 years ago, in India | The lion, about 1 million years ago, in Asia | From dogs, about 500 years ago, in old Europe
exercise: Why did early farmers welcome cats? | Cats caught mice in their grain stores | Cats guarded the sheep from wolves | Cats pulled the ploughs | Cats kept the farmers warm in winter by sleeping on their beds
exercise: Why does a cat push its whiskers into a gap first? | Its whiskers are about as wide as its body, so they tell it if it fits | To clean out the gap with its whiskers before it squeezes in | To smell what is inside | To scare mice out of the gap
exercise: Are cats always safe when they fall? | No, they can be badly hurt, so keep windows and balconies closed | Yes, they always land on their feet unhurt, from any height at all | Yes, they have nine lives | Only black cats get hurt
exercise: Why does a well-fed cat still hunt? | Hunting is an instinct from its wild ancestors | It is always secretly starving, however much it eats | It wants to buy more food | It is copying the dog
exercise: How many toes does a house cat usually have? | 18, five on each front paw and four on each back paw | 20, five on every paw | 16, four on every paw | 22, five on each front paw and six on each back paw
test: In a cartoon, a black panther is a different animal with no spots. What is the truth? | It is a dark leopard or jaguar, and its spots show in bright light | Black panthers are a kind of bear | It is right, panthers are their own kind of cat with no spots at all | It is a black lion
test: A zoo book shows big rosettes with spots inside and says leopard, from Africa. What is wrong? | Those rosettes belong to a jaguar, which lives in the Americas | Nothing, that is a leopard | Leopards have stripes, not spots | Leopards live only in the Americas, so it must be from South America
test: My mate says a cheetah can run 100 km per hour all day long. right? | No, it can sprint that fast only for about 20 to 30 seconds | Yes, it never gets tired | Yes, cheetahs run faster than planes and never need to stop | No, cheetahs walk everywhere
test: What would happen if a cheetah chased a gazelle for two whole minutes at top speed? | It would overheat and have to stop | It would run even faster | It would catch two gazelles | Nothing, cheetahs never tire
test: Why does a cheetah not roar at her cubs? | Cheetahs cannot roar; she chirps, and the cubs answer | She does not want lions to hear | Her cubs are deaf and cannot hear a roar at all | She roars only on Sundays
test: cat fell off the fence and landed on its feet, so cats can fall from anywhere yeah | No, they can still be badly hurt; keep windows and balconies shut | Yes, cats have nine lives | Yes, any height is fine | Only if the cat is young; old cats break legs
test: My cat brought a dead mouse to the back door, is she ill or something | No, cats are still hunters by instinct, even when well fed | Yes, cats only hunt when starving, so she needs more food | Yes, she wants to see a vet | No, she bought it at the shop
test: Count it: 2 house cats, how many toes in all? | 36 | 40 | 32 | 18
test: How many whiskers on 2 cats, about 24 each? | About 48 | About 24 | About 12 | About 96
test: A leopard, a jaguar and a cheetah. How many legs, hearts and tails? | 12 legs, 3 hearts and 3 tails | 12 legs, 6 hearts and 3 tails | Only 9 legs, 3 hearts and 3 tails | 12 legs, 3 hearts and no tails at all
test: What could a cat owner do to save birds in the garden? | Put a bell on the collar or keep the cat in at night | Feed the cat more and more food so that it stops hunting | Paint the birds a different colour | Nothing helps at all
test: Why do people think cats lived with humans at least 9,500 years ago? | A cat was found buried with a person on Cyprus from that time | Old cave paintings show cats sitting next to people at the fire | Cats told us in old stories | Cats appear in the oldest books
exam: Identify the land animal capable of attaining the greatest velocity. | The cheetah | The African lion | The leopard of Africa | The South American jaguar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called black panther? | A leopard or jaguar bearing excess dark pigment, its spots remaining discernible | A distinct species of cat, native to the jungle, entirely devoid of markings | A tiger in which the stripes have failed to develop | A hybrid of the lion and the domestic cat
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the leopard conveys its prey into the branches of trees. | To prevent the theft of the carcass by lions and hyenas | To permit the carcass to be dried by the wind | To conceal the carcass from rainfall | To attract other leopards so that they may share in the meal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the domestic cat. | It descends from the African wildcat, kept by humans for some ten thousand years | It descends from the tiger, domesticated by the peoples of India within the past century | It descends from the domestic dog, domesticated in antiquity | It descends from the lion, domesticated some million years ago
